{
 "lead": "A quadric surface is a curved shape whose points satisfy a second-degree equation in x, y, z. A few numbers give a sphere, ellipsoid, cone or cylinder instead of thousands of polygons. A blobby object works the other way: each centre spreads a soft density cloud, the clouds add, and the surface appears where the total crosses a threshold. Both store and hit-test curved shapes cheaply, in CAD parts, game props, ray tracers and cartoon gel.",
 "prereq": "You need <a href=\"#polygon-meshes-and-plane-equations\">Chapter 3.2</a> for surface normals and the plane equation, and Unit 2 for the translation that moves a centre.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Quadrics: second-degree surfaces",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A quadric is the set of points (x, y, z) that satisfy one polynomial equation of degree two."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Ax² + By² + Cz² + Dxy + Eyz + Fxz + Gx + Hy + Iz + J = 0",
     "where": "x, y, z are the point coordinates; A to J are constants, with at least one of A to F nonzero."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The coefficients decide the type. Centre the surface at the origin and drop the cross terms to get the standard forms below. For a moved surface, replace x by x − x₀ and so on."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Surface",
      "Implicit form",
      "Shape"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Sphere",
       "(x−x₀)² + (y−y₀)² + (z−z₀)² = r²",
       "closed spherical surface, radius r"
      ],
      [
       "Ellipsoid",
       "x²/a² + y²/b² + z²/c² = 1",
       "closed, semi-axes a, b, c"
      ],
      [
       "Elliptic paraboloid",
       "x²/a² + y²/b² = z",
       "open bowl"
      ],
      [
       "Hyperboloid, one sheet",
       "x²/a² + y²/b² − z²/c² = 1",
       "open tube"
      ],
      [
       "Hyperboloid, two sheets",
       "x²/a² − y²/b² − z²/c² = 1",
       "two cups"
      ],
      [
       "Elliptic cylinder",
       "x²/a² + y²/b² = 1",
       "open pipe, z free"
      ],
      [
       "Cone",
       "x²/a² + y²/b² − z²/c² = 0",
       "two cones meeting at a point"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "x = x₀ + r sin φ cos θ,  y = y₀ + r sin φ sin θ,  z = z₀ + r cos φ",
     "where": "θ is longitude in 0 ≤ θ ≤ 2π, φ is colatitude in 0 ≤ φ ≤ π, r is the radius. For an ellipsoid use a, b, c in place of r."
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "The torus (a ring) satisfies a degree-four equation, so it is <i>not</i> a quadric.",
      "Cones, cylinders, paraboloids and hyperboloids extend to infinity, so they must be clipped to a finite patch. The sphere and ellipsoid are bounded and need no clipping."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Blobby objects: a field cut at a threshold",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A blob, also called a metaball, comes from a density field. Each centre cᵢ spreads a soft cloud e^(−d²), where d is the distance from the point to cᵢ. The clouds add, and the visible surface is the level where the total equals a chosen threshold T."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "F(p) = Σᵢ e^(−|p − cᵢ|²) = T",
     "where": "p is a point in space, cᵢ is the i-th centre, T is the threshold. A larger T gives a smaller blob."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For one blob the sum is one term: e^(−r²) = T, so r = √(−ln T). At T = 0.5 this gives r = 0.833 units. Lowering T lets each blob grow."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Bring two centres close and their tails add. The field between them rises above T, and the separate blobs join with a smooth neck. That is why blobs suit water drops, jelly and cartoon characters. Superquadrics, which use |x|^n terms, are a cousin."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "figure": {
  "after_section": 1,
  "caption": "Along the line through the two blob centres, each blob alone crosses T = 0.5 at 0.833 units from its centre, the shaded bands. Halfway, at x = 2, the summed field is only 2 × e⁻⁴ = 0.037, far below 0.5, so the two blobs stay apart.",
