 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "(a) Write the implicit equation of the sphere of radius 5 centred at (2, 3, 1), expanded into general quadric form. (b) Two blobs sit at (0,0,0) and (4,0,0), with density e^(−d²) and threshold T = 0.5. Find the radius of a single blob and the field value at the midpoint (2,0,0), and decide whether the blobs merge.",
   "steps": [
    "Sphere form: (x − 2)² + (y − 3)² + (z − 1)² = 25.",
    "Expand: x² − 4x + 4 + y² − 6y + 9 + z² − 2z + 1 = 25.",
    "Collect: x² + y² + z² − 4x − 6y − 2z + 14 − 25 = 0.",
    "So x² + y² + z² − 4x − 6y − 2z − 11 = 0, that is A = B = C = 1, G = −4, H = −6, I = −2, J = −11.",
    "Single blob: e^(−r²) = 0.5 gives r² = 0.693, so r = 0.833 units.",
    "Midpoint (2,0,0) is distance 2 from each centre, so each term is e^(−4) = 0.01832.",
    "Sum at the midpoint: 0.01832 + 0.01832 = 0.0366, far below T = 0.5.",
    "The midpoint lies outside the surface, so the two blobs stay separate."
   ],
   "answer": "Sphere x² + y² + z² − 4x − 6y − 2z − 11 = 0; single blob radius 0.833 units; midpoint field 0.037, below 0.5, so the blobs stay separate.",
   "check": "import math\nprint(4 + 9 + 1 - 25)\nr2 = -math.log(0.5)\nprint(round(r2, 3))\nprint(round(math.sqrt(r2), 3))\nprint(round(2 * math.exp(-4), 3))\n",
   "values": [
    -11,
    0.693,
    0.833,
    0.037
   ],
   "output": ""
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Calling the torus a quadric. A ring needs a degree-four equation.",
  "Forgetting to clip an unbounded quadric. A cone or cylinder then fills the whole scene.",
  "Confusing the threshold with the radius. The radius comes from r = √(−ln T), and a smaller T gives a bigger blob.",
  "A sign slip while expanding. The constant term is x₀² + y₀² + z₀² − r², here −11, not +11.",
  "Treating two blobs as independent spheres. The field is a sum, so the surface bulges toward the other centre and merges when the midpoint value reaches T."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "State the general equation of a quadric surface and give its degree.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "Ax² + By² + Cz² + Dxy + Eyz + Fxz + Gx + Hy + Iz + J = 0. It is degree two."
  },
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Give the implicit equation of a sphere of radius r centred at (x₀, y₀, z₀).",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "(x − x₀)² + (y − y₀)² + (z − z₀)² = r²."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why do some quadrics need clipping before they are drawn?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Unbounded quadrics such as cones, cylinders, paraboloids and hyperboloids extend to infinity. Without clipping, a cone or cylinder covers the whole scene. The sphere and ellipsoid are bounded and need no clipping."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A single blob has density e^(−d²). Find its radius for threshold T = 0.25.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "e^(−r²) = 0.25, so r² = ln 4 = 1.386 and r = 1.177 units."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "Expand the sphere of radius 3 centred at (−1, 2, 0) into general quadric form.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "(x + 1)² + (y − 2)² + z² = 9, so x² + y² + z² + 2x − 4y + (1 + 4 − 9) = 0, that is x² + y² + z² + 2x − 4y − 4 = 0."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Two blobs of density e^(−d²) with T = 0.5 move from 4 units apart to 1 unit apart. Describe the change in the surface.",
   "marks": 5,
   "a": "At 4 units apart the midpoint field is 2e^(−4) = 0.037 < 0.5, so there are two separate blobs. At 1 unit apart the midpoint field is 2e^(−0.25) = 1.558 > 0.5, so the two become one merged surface with a neck between the centres."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. Modelling tools such as Blender and Maya build metaballs this way, then convert them to triangles with marching cubes, which samples the field on a grid and stitches triangles where the samples cross T. Fonts on your phone are drawn from signed distance fields, a cousin of this density field, for sharp edges at any size. Ray marchers step through the same sum of blobs without building a mesh."
}